        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.495,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.111,
            0.881,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": "**Example 5:** Here is a statement of the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras theorem."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.149,
            0.881,
            0.188
          ],
          "content": "Let $a , b , c$ be the sidelengths of a triangle. If the triangle is right-angled, then $a ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 2 } = c ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.188,
            0.388,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": "What is its converse?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.207,
            0.881,
            0.245
          ],
          "content": "To write the converse, we keep the first sentence of the Baudhāyana– Pythagoras theorem as it is, and make the necessary change in the second."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.244,
            0.881,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": "Let a, b, c be the sidelengths of a triangle. If $a ^ { 2 }   +   b ^ { 2 }   =   c ^ { 2 } ,$ then the triangle is right-angled."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.284,
            0.389,
            0.3
          ],
          "content": "Is this converse true?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.3,
            0.878,
            0.321
          ],
          "content": "Let $\\Delta \\mathrm { A B C }$ be the triangle with sidelengths a, b, c such that $a^{2} + b^{2} = c^{2}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.369,
            0.327,
            0.633,
            0.505
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.533,
            0.851,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": "Construct another right triangle ∆XYZ whose perpendicular sides YZ and XZ have sidelengths a and b."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.575,
            0.629,
            0.739
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.744,
            0.559,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": "What can we say about the length of XY?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.765,
            0.658,
            0.783
          ],
          "content": "(**Hint:** Use the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras Theorem.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.784,
            0.557,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": "What can we say about ∆ABC and ∆XYZ?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.802,
            0.596,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": "(**Hint:** Use the SSS criterian for congruence.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.822,
            0.737,
            0.84
          ],
          "content": "We conclude that ∆ABC had to be a right triangle after all."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.84,
            0.88,
            0.86
          ],
          "content": "Hence the converse of the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras Theorem is also true!"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
